BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Hamilton Lugar School": "https://events.iu.edu/hls/event/2160889-the-alchemy-of-confidence"}
X-SOURCE-ID:iu_hamilton_lugar
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
SUMMARY:The Alchemy of Confidence
DTSTART:20260421T200000Z
DTEND:20260421T213000Z
DTSTAMP:20260106T152437Z
UID:20260421T200000Z-2160889@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260421T200000Z
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:This salon series at Indiana University features leading think
 ers exploring how to build better digital and natural worlds.Beyond the We
 b / After Analog Speaker Series.Why do we trust some signals—and fall fo
 r others?  Deception and honesty have long been locked in evolutionary arm
 s races. From peacock feathers to social media profiles\, signals shape ho
 w we judge honesty and deception. But when false signals become widespread
 \, trust breaks down—and only signals backed by real cost remain credibl
 e.  This talk introduces signaling theory\, a powerful framework for under
 standing:Why costly behaviors signal authenticity  How deception spreads i
 n human communication  What this means for disinformation and identity in 
 the digital age  How can technologies support trust and when does it under
 mine it? As rapid technological change reshapes the incentives for decepti
 on and detection\, understanding the foundations of trust has never been m
 ore urgent.  Beyond signals alone\, we'll explore how trust\, social syste
 ms\, and oversight determine what—and who—we believe.About the Speaker
   Judith is a writer\, designer and artist whose work focuses on the co-ev
 olution of technology and society. She has published numerous articles abo
 ut social media\, AI\, ethics and anonymity\, and is the author of The Soc
 ial Machine: Designs for Living Online (http://amzn.to/NXFIFt) (MIT Press)
 . As the former director of the MIT Media Lab's Sociable Media Group\, she
  and her students designed innovative interfaces for on-line communities\;
  their art projects examining the future of identity\, privacy and mediate
 d life have been exhibited in museums and galleries worldwide.&#160\;
GEO:39.169199;-86.515674
LAST-MODIFIED:20260406T170437Z
LOCATION:STEPHEN L. &amp\; CONNIE J. FERGUSON INTERNATIONAL CENTER - Room 
 Number: 421/423
URL:https://events.iu.edu/hls/event/2160889-the-alchemy-of-confidence
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  This salon series at Indiana University 
 features leading thinkers exploring how to build better digital and natura
 l worlds.</p><p><img src="https://vivatropolis.com/judith/JSD.CroppedSm  a
 ll.png" alt="" loading="lazy"/></p><p><strong data-olk-copy-source="Messag
 eBody">Beyond the Web / After Analog Speaker Series.</strong></p><p data-s
 tart="131" data-end="466"><strong><em>Why do we trust some signals—and f
 all for others?</em></strong></p><p data-start="148" data-end="359">  Dece
 ption and honesty have long been locked in evolutionary arms races. From p
 eacock feathers to social media profiles\, signals shape how we judge hone
 sty and deception. But when false signals become widespread\, trust breaks
  down—and only signals backed by real cost remain credible.</p><p data-s
 tart="361" data-end="443">  This talk introduces <strong data-start="382" 
 data-end="402">signaling theory</strong>\, a powerful framework for unders
 tanding:</p><ul data-start="444" data-end="610"><li data-section-id="ukm1n
 s" data-start="444" data-end="488">Why costly behaviors signal authenticit
 y  </li><li data-section-id="di6nl5" data-start="489" data-end="537">How d
 eception spreads in human communication  </li><li data-section-id="hwujz7"
  data-start="538" data-end="610">What this means for <strong data-start="5
 60" data-end="610">disinformation and identity in the digital age</strong>
 </li></ul><p data-start="778" data-end="1319" data-is-last-node="">  How c
 an technologies support trust and when does it undermine it? As rapid tech
 nological change reshapes the incentives for deception and detection\, und
 erstanding the foundations of trust has never been more urgent.</p><p data
 -start="778" data-end="1319" data-is-last-node="">  Beyond signals alone\,
  we'll explore how <strong data-start="652" data-end="692">trust\, social 
 systems\, and oversight</strong> determine what—and who—we believe.</p
 ><p data-start="778" data-end="1319" data-is-last-node=""><strong>About th
 e Speaker</strong></p><p data-start="778" data-end="1319" data-is-last-nod
 e="">  Judith is a writer\, designer and artist whose work focuses on the 
 co-evolution of technology and society. She has published numerous article
 s about social media\, AI\, ethics and anonymity\, and is the author of <i
 ><a href="http://amzn.to/NXFIFt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Social
  Machine: Designs for Living Online</a></i> (MIT Press). As the former dir
 ector of the MIT Media Lab's Sociable Media Group\, she and her students d
 esigned innovative interfaces for on-line communities\; their art projects
  examining the future of identity\, privacy and mediated life have been ex
 hibited in museums and galleries worldwide.&#160\;</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  <strong>Questions? Contact</strong>: intia
 sar@iu.edu\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CUSTOM-ROOM-NUMBER:421/423
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2160889
X-LIVEWHALE-IS-ONLINE:1
X-LIVEWHALE-ONLINE-INSTRUCTIONS:<div>\n  <span data-olk-copy-source="Messa
 geBody">Join from computer or mobile:</span>\n</div>\n<div>\n  <a class="m
 s-outlook-linkify" title="https://iu.zoom.us/j/86134171048" href="https://
 iu.zoom.us/j/86134171048" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-linkindex="0
 ">https://iu.zoom.us/j/86134171048</a>\n</div>
X-LIVEWHALE-ONLINE-TYPE:Hybrid
X-LIVEWHALE-ONLINE-URL:https://iu.zoom.us/j/86134171048
X-LIVEWHALE-RSVP:1
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<div id="lw_cal_event_detail_intro" class="lw_cal_even
 t_detail_block">\n  <p>\n    Join us for an exciting talk given by Hardvar
 d faculty fellow <strong>Judith Donath</strong>&#160\;as part of the "Beyo
 nd the Web" salon series piloted by Ostrom Workshop @ IU and the Hamilton 
 Lugar School.\n  </p>\n</div>
