X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Hamilton Lugar School": "https://events.iu.edu/hls/event/2221418-frontlines-futures-graduate-foreign-policy", "IU Kelley School of Business": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2221418-frontlines-futures-graduate-foreign-policy"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Hamilton Lugar School": "https://events.iu.edu/hls/event/2221418-frontlines-futures-graduate-foreign-policy"}
X-SOURCE-ID:iu_hamilton_lugar
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School, IU Kelley School of Business
SUMMARY:Frontlines & Futures: Graduate Foreign Policy Salon Series: Reform
 ing the United Nations: Can Multilateral Governance Evolve?
DTSTART:20260422T160000Z
DTEND:20260422T173000Z
DTSTAMP:20260225T135206Z
UID:20260422T160000Z-2221418@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260422T160000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/135/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,1024,1536/28558_Indiana_University_Foreign_Policy_Event.r
 ev.1759952589.png
CATEGORIES:Career development,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences,T
 raining, non-credit classes, and learning opportunities
DESCRIPTION:This session invites graduate students\, alumni\, faculty\, st
 aff\, and the general public to explore how the United Nations can evolve 
 in response to emerging global powers\, technological innovation\, and shi
 fting alliances. Participants will examine challenges like climate-induced
  migration\, artificial intelligence\, cyber threats\, and the rise of new
  economic blocs\, assessing how these trends impact U.S. interests and glo
 bal governance.  Through guided discussion\, reflection\, and visual promp
 ts\, participants will debate bold policy strategies\, consider ethical tr
 ade-offs\, and propose actionable reforms to strengthen multilateral insti
 tutions. Questions will include: Which emerging trend or power could most 
 reshape global politics? How should the U.S. respond to protect both natio
 nal and global priorities? What bold policy steps could strengthen multila
 teral governance\, and how can allies be coordinated to build trust? How s
 hould ethical principles guide decisions around emerging technologies and 
 global crises?  This safe\, structured space emphasizes candid dialogue\, 
 critical thinking\, and real-world policy analysis. Lunch will be provided
 . Join us for the final session of the academic year to sharpen your forei
 gn policy instincts and engage with peers on the pressing issues shaping t
 omorrow's global landscape.
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20260331T193116Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING - Room Number: 3015
URL:https://events.iu.edu/hls/event/2221418-frontlines-futures-graduate-fo
 reign-policy
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p data-start="220" data-end="659">  This ses
 sion invites graduate students\, alumni\, faculty\, staff\, and the genera
 l public to explore how the United Nations can evolve in response to emerg
 ing global powers\, technological innovation\, and shifting alliances. Par
 ticipants will examine challenges like climate-induced migration\, artific
 ial intelligence\, cyber threats\, and the rise of new economic blocs\, as
 sessing how these trends impact U.S. interests and global governance.</p><
 p data-start="661" data-end="1246">  Through guided discussion\, reflectio
 n\, and visual prompts\, participants will debate bold policy strategies\,
  consider ethical trade-offs\, and propose actionable reforms to strengthe
 n multilateral institutions. Questions will include: Which emerging trend 
 or power could most reshape global politics? How should the U.S. respond t
 o protect both national and global priorities? What bold policy steps coul
 d strengthen multilateral governance\, and how can allies be coordinated t
 o build trust? How should ethical principles guide decisions around emergi
 ng technologies and global crises?</p><p data-start="1248" data-end="1552"
 >  This safe\, structured space emphasizes candid dialogue\, critical thin
 king\, and real-world policy analysis. Lunch will be provided. Join us for
  the final session of the academic year to sharpen your foreign policy ins
 tincts and engage with peers on the pressing issues shaping tomorrow's glo
 bal landscape.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<div>\n  <strong>Katherine A. Ntiamoah<br />\n  k
 ntiamoa@iu.edu</strong>\n</div>\n<div>\n  Director of Policy Engagement an
 d Strategic Partnerships\n</div>\n<div>\n  Hamilton Lugar School of Global
  &amp\; International Studies\n</div>
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
X-LIVEWHALE-CUSTOM-ROOM-NUMBER:3015
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2221418
