SUMMARY:Engs Lecture 2026: The Poison Book Project
DTSTART:20260422T203000Z
DTEND:20260422T213000Z
DTSTAMP:20260413T103642Z
UID:20260422T203000Z-2266989@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260422T203000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,864,864/30806_engs_lecture_2026_LW_avatar.rev.1775489621.
 jpg
CATEGORIES:Art and museum exhibitions,Lectures, talks, seminars, and confe
 rences
DESCRIPTION:Join us at IU Libraries Lilly Library on Wednesday\, April 22\
 , 2026 for the annual Ruth C. Engs Lecture on Public Health. We are excite
 d to welcome this year's speakers\, Dr. Melissa Tedone and Dr. Rosie Grayb
 urn\, Lead Conservator and Conservation Scientist for the Poison Book Proj
 ect. The speakers will discuss their research focusing on identifying pote
 ntially toxic pigments used in bookbinding components and how to handle an
 d store potentially toxic collections more safely.  Along with refreshment
 s\, visitors will have the opportunity to explore the history of science a
 nd healthcare through a hands-on pop-up exhibition of some of the Lilly Li
 brary's renowned historical medical materials.  Schedule4:30 p.m.: Pop-up 
 exhibition featuring arsenical books from the Lilly Library's collections 
 as well as materials related to the history of medicine and public health5
 :30 p.m.: The Poison Book Project: The Hidden Hazard on Your Bookshelves\,
  the 2026 Ruth C. Engs lecture\, delivered by Dr. Melissa Tedone and Dr. R
 osie Grayburn6:30 p.m.: Reception and pop-up exhibition  About the speaker
 sMelissa Tedone is Assistant Professor for Library &amp\; Archives Conserv
 ation in the Department of Art Conservation\, University of Delaware. She 
 also serves as associate director of the master's level Winterthur/Univers
 ity of Delaware Program in Art Conservation (WUDPAC). Her primary research
  focuses on the industrialization of the Victorian bookbinding trade and h
 azards in library and archives collections.Rosie Grayburn is Conservation 
 Scientist/Head of the Scientific Research &amp\; Analysis Laboratory at Wi
 nterthur Museum\, Garden &amp\; Library. She is an affiliated associate pr
 ofessor in WUDPAC\, where she teaches conservation science and analytical 
 methodologies to graduate fellows in art conservation. Her main areas of r
 esearch are hazards in collections\, the optimization and study of treatme
 nts for silver and silverplate\, and the greening of organic solvents used
  in conservation practice.  Together\, Drs. Tedone and Grayburn started th
 e Poison Book Project in 2019 and founded the international Bibliotoxicolo
 gy Working Group (BibTox) in 2022.  Stay in the loop  Keep up with events 
 and exhibitions at the Lilly Library by subscribing to our emails (https:/
 /go.iu.edu/lilly-news).
GEO:39.167909;-86.518974
LAST-MODIFIED:20260413T143642Z
LOCATION:LILLY LIBRARY
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2259790-engs-lecture-2026-the-poison
 -book-project
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Join us at IU Libraries Lilly Library on
  <strong>Wednesday\, April 22\, 2026</strong> for the annual Ruth C. Engs 
 Lecture on Public Health. We are excited to welcome this year's speakers\,
  <strong>Dr. Melissa Tedone and Dr. Rosie Grayburn\,</strong> Lead Conserv
 ator and Conservation Scientist for the <strong>Poison Book Project</stron
 g>. The speakers will discuss their research focusing on identifying poten
 tially toxic pigments used in bookbinding components and how to handle and
  store potentially toxic collections more safely.</p><p>  Along with refre
 shments\, visitors will have the opportunity to explore the history of sci
 ence and healthcare through a hands-on pop-up exhibition of some of the Li
 lly Library's renowned historical medical materials.</p><h2>  Schedule</h2
 ><p><strong>4:30 p.m.</strong>: Pop-up exhibition featuring arsenical book
 s from the Lilly Library's collections as well as materials related to the
  history of medicine and public health</p><p><strong>5:30 p.m.</strong>: <
 i>The Poison Book Project: The Hidden Hazard on Your Bookshelves</i>\, the
  2026 Ruth C. Engs lecture\, delivered by Dr. Melissa Tedone and Dr. Rosie
  Grayburn</p><p><strong>6:30 p.m.</strong>: Reception and pop-up exhibitio
 n</p><h2>  About the speakers</h2><p><strong>Melissa Tedone</strong> is As
 sistant Professor for Library &amp\; Archives Conservation in the Departme
 nt of Art Conservation\, University of Delaware. She also serves as associ
 ate director of the master's level Winterthur/University of Delaware Progr
 am in Art Conservation (WUDPAC). Her primary research focuses on the indus
 trialization of the Victorian bookbinding trade and hazards in library and
  archives collections.</p><p><strong>Rosie Grayburn</strong> is Conservati
 on Scientist/Head of the Scientific Research &amp\; Analysis Laboratory at
  Winterthur Museum\, Garden &amp\; Library. She is an affiliated associate
  professor in WUDPAC\, where she teaches conservation science and analytic
 al methodologies to graduate fellows in art conservation. Her main areas o
 f research are hazards in collections\, the optimization and study of trea
 tments for silver and silverplate\, and the greening of organic solvents u
 sed in conservation practice.</p><p>  Together\, Drs. Tedone and Grayburn 
 started the Poison Book Project in 2019 and founded the international Bibl
 iotoxicology Working Group (BibTox) in 2022.</p><h2>  Stay in the loop</h2
 ><p>  Keep up with events and exhibitions at the Lilly Library by <a href=
 "https://go.iu.edu/lilly-news" target="_blank" rel="noopener">subscribing 
 to our emails</a>.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  <strong>Lilly Library</strong><br />\n  <a
  href="mailto:liblilly@iu.edu">liblilly@iu.edu<br />\n  <br /></a><strong>
 Erin Chiparo</strong><br />\n  Silver-Norman Curator of Dermatology\, Gene
 ral Medicine\, and Science<br />\n  <a href="mailto:echiparo@iu.edu">echip
 aro@iu.edu<br />\n  <br /></a>\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
