BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Bloomington Libraries": "https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2193032-making-indiana-university"}
X-SOURCE-ID:iu_libraries
X-SOURCE:IU Bloomington Libraries
SUMMARY:Making Indiana University: A Reading\, Conversation\, and Signing 
 with Jim Capshew
DTSTART:20260423T213000Z
DTEND:20260423T233000Z
DTSTAMP:20260129T171528Z
UID:20260423T213000Z-2193032@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260423T213000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,2550,3300/30226_cover1-4.rev.1772040814.png
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:Blending institutional and environmental history\, Making Indi
 ana University: History\, Landscape\, and a Sense of Place (https://iulibr
 ariespublishing.iu.edu/making-IU/) traces how the university's identity ha
 s been shaped—materially and imaginatively—by Dunn's Woods\, the evolv
 ing campus landscape\, and the narratives through which generations have u
 nderstood this place.  In a setting central to IU's early history\, author
  Jim Capshew (https://hpsc.indiana.edu/about/emeriti-faculty/capshew-james
 .html) will introduce the book\, read from it\, and take questions from th
 e audience. The event will be introduced by John Summerlot\, Director of E
 nterprise Risk &amp\; Incident Management at the IU Foundation. It will co
 nclude with a signing of a specially produced booklet featuring the Wylie 
 House chapter\, along with commemorative image cards drawn from the volume
 . Following the program\, guests are invited to enjoy a self-guided tour o
 f the Wylie House.  Light refreshments will be served.  The event also cel
 ebrates IU Libraries Publishing (https://libraries.indiana.edu/libraries-p
 ublishing)\, the university's new open-access publisher of long-form schol
 arly\, creative\, and public-facing works. By making publications freely a
 vailable worldwide at no cost to readers or authors\, IU Libraries Publish
 ing advances Indiana University's public mission and ensures that its scho
 larship remains accessible to all.  This event will be of particular inter
 est to IU alumni\, Bloomington residents\, faculty and staff\, and anyone 
 interested in university history\, environmental studies\, and the evolvin
 g story of Indiana University.  Doors will open at 5 p.m. This event is fr
 ee and open to the public.  Free parking options are available for visitor
 s:Neighborhood Street Parking: Free street parking is available after 5 p.
 m.\, including in areas normally restricted to Neighborhood Zone permit ho
 lders.  Wylie House Museum Parking: Complimentary parking (no permit requi
 red) is available behind the Wylie House Museum and adjacent to its Educat
 ion Center (the gray barn). This lot includes one accessible parking space
 . Access these spaces via the alley behind the museum\, from Grant Street 
 and Lincoln Street.
