BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"WFHB Community Calendar": "https://wfhb.org/event/author-event-with-david-george/?instance_id=94729"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"WFHB Community Calendar": "https://wfhb.org/event/author-event-with-david-george/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:wfhb
X-SOURCE:WFHB Community Calendar
SUMMARY:Author event with David George
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260424T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260424T190000
DTSTAMP:20260421T020832Z
UID:ai1ec-148028@wfhb.org
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=America/New_York:20260424T180000
SEQUENCE:0
CATEGORIES:
CONTACT:
DESCRIPTION:We invite you to join us for an evening with biologist and aut
 hor David George Haskell in celebration of his new book\, How Flowers Made
  Our World: The Story of Nature’s Revolutionaries! For this event\, Davi
 d will be in conversation with IU biology professor Jen Lau.\nHosted in co
 ordination with IU’s Environmental Futures in the Arts and Humanities in
 itiative.\nABOUT THE BOOK:\nAn exquisite exploration of the power of flowe
 rs\, placing them at the center of the story of how evolution created the 
 world we know today.\nLooking to the future\, flowers offer us lessons on 
 resilience and creativity in the face of rapid environmental change. We ne
 ed floral creativity\, beauty\, and joy more than ever. How Flowers Made O
 ur World combines lyrical writing\, sensual exploration\, and the latest i
 n scientific research to explore some of the most consequential life forms
  ever to have evolved\, showing how our planet came to be and how it thriv
 es today.
LOCATION:Morgenstern Books & Cafe\, 849 South Auto Mall Drive\, Bloomingto
 n
URL:https://wfhb.org/event/author-event-with-david-george/
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC '-//W3C//DTD HTML 3.2//
 EN'>\n<HTML>\n<HEAD>\n<TITLE></TITLE>\n</HEAD>\n<BODY><p dir='ltr'>We invi
 te you to join us for an evening with biologist and author David George Ha
 skell in celebration of his new book\, <em>How Flowers Made Our World: The
  Story of Nature’s Revolutionaries!</em> For this event\, David will be 
 in conversation with IU biology professor Jen Lau.</p>\n<p dir='ltr'>Hoste
 d in coordination with IU’s <a href='https://ahfutures.iu.edu/environmen
 tal-futures/index.html' target='_blank' rel='noopener' data-saferedirectur
 l='https://www.google.com/url?q=https://ahfutures.iu.edu/environmental-fut
 ures/index.html&source=gmail&ust=1775587539144000&usg=AOvVaw1qw3tXYPhMuFxL
 J5t4WY8W'>Environmental Futures in the Arts and Humanities initiative</a>.
 </p>\n<p dir='ltr'>ABOUT THE BOOK:</p>\n<p dir='ltr'>An exquisite explorat
 ion of the power of flowers\, placing them at the center of the story of h
 ow evolution created the world we know today.</p>\n<p>Looking to the futur
 e\, flowers offer us lessons on resilience and creativity in the face of r
 apid environmental change. We need floral creativity\, beauty\, and joy mo
 re than ever. <em>How Flowers Made Our World</em> combines lyrical writing
 \, sensual exploration\, and the latest in scientific research to explore 
 some of the most consequential life forms ever to have evolved\, showing h
 ow our planet came to be and how it thrives today.</p>\n</BODY></HTML>
X-COST-TYPE:free
END:VEVENT
