BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Arts & Humanities Institute": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2238168-a-lecture-by-emily-zazulia"}
X-SOURCE-ID:iu_arts_humanities
X-SOURCE:IU Arts & Humanities Institute
SUMMARY:Emily Zazulia: "Music for the eyes? Rethinking 'Augenmusik'"
DTSTART:20260430T200000Z
DTEND:20260430T220000Z
DTSTAMP:20260313T094433Z
UID:20260430T200000Z-2238169@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260430T200000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/150/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,768,768/30503_zazulia-photo.rev.1773408992.jpg
CATEGORIES:Culture and languages,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conference
 s
DESCRIPTION:Abstract: Late fifteenth-century music occasionally asks perfo
 rmers to read its notation in unusual ways— from slowing down a melodic 
 line to turning it backwards or upside-down\, or even omitting certain not
 es or rests. These visual elements are integral dimensions of the work's c
 onceptual whole. Yet they have never sat easily with music scholars and cr
 itics for whom music is\, by definition\, that which reaches the ears. In 
 1912\, Alfred Einstein gave this discomfort a name:Augenmusik\, music for 
 the eyes.Einstein's coinage\, it turns out\, says more about 1912 than abo
 ut 1480. This paper traces the historiography of that term\, arguing that 
 each successive framework—from Einstein's formalist dismissal to the mod
 ernist reclamation of the score as visual object—has projected its own a
 ssumptions about the relationship between the senses onto a repertoire tha
 t operated by a different logic entirely. That logic\, I argue\, was intel
 lectual rather than sensory: for fifteenth-century musicians\, engaging wi
 th notation was not a deviation from aesthetic experience but a form of it
 \, a prized dimension of what it meant to encounter a piece of music.Augen
 musikearns its place\, finally\, as an admission: that notation\, here\, w
 as doing something the modern discipline had no other language for.Emily Z
 azulia (https://music.berkeley.edu/people/emily-zazulia)is Associate Profe
 ssor of Music at the University of California\, Berkeley. Her first monogr
 aphWhere Sight Meets Sound: The Poetics of Late-Medieval Music Writing(Oxf
 ord\, 2021) received the Early Music Award from the American Musicological
  Society and the Emerging Scholar Book Award from the Society for Music Th
 eory. She has received other awards and fellowships from the National Endo
 wment for the Humanities\, the American Council of Learned Societies\, the
  American Musicological Society\, the Medieval Academy of America\, the Re
 naissance Society of America\, Harvard University's Villa I Tatti\, and th
 e Hellman Foundation. Current major projects include a monograph on popula
 r song and ideas of the secular\, a co-edited volume on the composer Josqu
 in des Prez\, and a digital project on Mapping the Musical Renaissance.Emi
 ly Zazulia's visit to Indiana University is made possible through generous
  support from the College of Arts and Sciences\, the Department of Musicol
 ogy\, and the Department of Music Theory.
GEO:39.16705;-86.524718
LAST-MODIFIED:20260413T171109Z
LOCATION:MAXWELL HALL - Room Number: Grand Hall
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2238168-a-lecture-by-emily-zazulia
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><span data-contrast="none">Abstract</span>
 <span data-contrast="none">: Late fifteenth-century music occasionally ask
 s performers to read its notation in unusual ways— from slowing down a m
 elodic line to turning it backwards or upside-down\, or even omitting cert
 ain notes or rests. These visual elements are integral dimensions of the w
 ork's conceptual whole. Yet they have never sat easily with music scholars
  and critics for whom music is\, by definition\, that which reaches the ea
 rs. In 1912\, Alfred Einstein gave this discomfort a name:</span><i><span 
 data-contrast="none">Augenmusik</span></i><span data-contrast="none">\, mu
 sic for the eyes.</span><span data-ccp-props="{}"></span></p><p><span data
 -contrast="none">Einstein's coinage\, it turns out\, says more about 1912 
 than about 1480. This paper traces the historiography of that term\, argui
 ng that each successive framework—from Einstein's formalist dismissal to
  the modernist reclamation of the score as visual object—has projected i
 ts own assumptions about the relationship between the senses onto a repert
 oire that operated by a different logic entirely. That logic\, I argue\, w
 as intellectual rather than sensory: for fifteenth-century musicians\, eng
 aging with notation was not a deviation from aesthetic experience but a fo
 rm of it\, a prized dimension of what it meant to encounter a piece of mus
 ic.</span><i><span data-contrast="none">Augenmusik</span></i><span data-co
 ntrast="none">earns its place\, finally\, as an admission: that notation\,
  here\, was doing something the modern discipline had no other language fo
 r.</span><span data-ccp-props="{}"></span></p><p><span data-contrast="auto
 "><a class="external" href="https://music.berkeley.edu/people/emily-zazuli
 a">Emily Zazulia</a></span><span data-contrast="auto">is Associate Profess
 or of Music at the University of California\, Berkeley. Her first monograp
 h</span><i><span data-contrast="auto">Where Sight Meets Sound: The Poetics
  of Late-Medieval Music Writing</span></i><span data-contrast="auto">(Oxfo
 rd\, 2021) received the Early Music Award from the American Musicological 
 Society and the Emerging Scholar Book Award from the Society for Music The
 ory. She has received other awards and fellowships from the National Endow
 ment for the Humanities\, the American Council of Learned Societies\, the 
 American Musicological Society\, the Medieval Academy of America\, the Ren
 aissance Society of America\, Harvard University's Villa I Tatti\, and the
  Hellman Foundation. Current major projects include a monograph on popular
  song and ideas of the secular\, a co-edited volume on the composer Josqui
 n des Prez\, and a digital project on Mapping the Musical Renaissance.</sp
 an><span data-ccp-props="{}"></span></p><p><i><span data-contrast="none">E
 mily Zazulia's visit to Indiana University is made possible through genero
 us support from the College of Arts and Sciences\, the Department of Music
 ology\, and the Department of Music Theory.</span></i><span data-ccp-props
 ="{}"></span></p>
