X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Hamilton Lugar School": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2178816-us-economic-cooperation-with-nations-in-the-middle", "IU Kelley School of Business": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2178816-us-economic-cooperation-with-nations-in-the-middle"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Hamilton Lugar School": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2178816-us-economic-cooperation-with-nations-in-the-middle"}
X-SOURCE-ID:iu_hamilton_lugar
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School, IU Kelley School of Business
SUMMARY:U.S. Economic Cooperation with Nations in the Middle East and Cent
 ral Asia: Working Towards Regional Stabilities
DTSTART:20260430T210000Z
DTEND:20260430T221500Z
DTSTAMP:20260121T112740Z
UID:20260430T210000Z-2180110@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260430T210000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/176/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,0,0/29606_KhushChoksy.avif
DESCRIPTION:Nations of the Middle East and Central Asia have emerged over 
 the last decade as important partners to the United States both economical
 ly and strategically. Economic ties have expanded with the United States t
 hrough two-way direct investments\, third country cooperation\, collaborat
 ion in innovation technologies and supply chain diversifications. At the s
 ame time\, the economic integration of these two regions has also increase
 d\, with both state and private investment from the GCC countries into Tur
 key and Central Asia. In the geopolitical sphere\, Middle Eastern nations 
 seek to maintain good relationships with China and Russia\, while enhancin
 g ties with Washington. Central Asian countries seek to expand political a
 nd economic links with the United States to diversify from Russia and Chin
 a. This discussion will focus on the business and economic ties between Am
 erica and these regions as they look toward regional stability and global 
 economic competitiveness.  &#160\;  Khush Choksy is senior vice president 
 of International Member Relations at the U.S. Chamber of Commerce. In this
  capacity\, he oversees member engagement and fundraising across the Inter
 national Division and its over 30 business councils and programs. He also 
 leads the Chamber's programs in the Middle East\, Türkiye\, and Central A
 sia.  As the head of International Member Relations\, Choksy is responsibl
 e for member services across all the U.S. Chamber's companies that are par
 t of its International Division.  As senior vice president of the Middle E
 ast and Türkiye Department\, Choksy's responsibilities include oversight 
 of several bilateral business councils and programs. Under his leadership\
 , the Middle East portfolio has expanded to become the U.S. Chamber's larg
 est international program and represents over 250 companies. Choksy also l
 ed the effort to integrate the U.S.-India Business Council into the U.S. C
 hamber.  Before joining the Chamber\, Choksy was a director at Chemonics I
 nternational. There\, he led teams developing economic ties between the Un
 ited States and the Middle East. He lived and worked in Jordan for seven y
 ears\, and also lived and worked in India\, Sri Lanka\, and the U.K. Prior
  to that\, Choksy worked for the Parsons Corporation. He is a recognized t
 hought leader on the role of international business in the global economy.
   Choksy received his M.B.A. in strategic management from the Wharton Scho
 ol of the University of Pennsylvania and his B.A.\, magna cum laude\, from
  Hamilton College. He lives with his wife and daughter in Chevy Chase\, Ma
 ryland.
LAST-MODIFIED:20260408T174557Z
LOCATION:GISB - Room Number: 3067
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2178816-us-economic-cooperation-with
 -nations-in-the-middle
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Nations of the Middle East and Central A
 sia have emerged over the last decade as important partners to the United 
 States both economically and strategically. Economic ties have expanded wi
 th the United States through two-way direct investments\, third country co
 operation\, collaboration in innovation technologies and supply chain dive
 rsifications. At the same time\, the economic integration of these two reg
 ions has also increased\, with both state and private investment from the 
 GCC countries into Turkey and Central Asia. In the geopolitical sphere\, M
 iddle Eastern nations seek to maintain good relationships with China and R
 ussia\, while enhancing ties with Washington. Central Asian countries seek
  to expand political and economic links with the United States to diversif
 y from Russia and China. This discussion will focus on the business and ec
 onomic ties between America and these regions as they look toward regional
  stability and global economic competitiveness.</p><p>  &#160\;</p><p>  Kh
 ush Choksy is senior vice president of International Member Relations at t
 he U.S. Chamber of Commerce. In this capacity\, he oversees member engagem
 ent and fundraising across the International Division and its over 30 busi
 ness councils and programs. He also leads the Chamber's programs in the Mi
 ddle East\, Türkiye\, and Central Asia.</p><p>  As the head of Internatio
 nal Member Relations\, Choksy is responsible for member services across al
 l the U.S. Chamber's companies that are part of its International Division
 .</p><p>  As senior vice president of the Middle East and Türkiye Departm
 ent\, Choksy's responsibilities include oversight of several bilateral bus
 iness councils and programs. Under his leadership\, the Middle East portfo
 lio has expanded to become the U.S. Chamber's largest international progra
 m and represents over 250 companies. Choksy also led the effort to integra
 te the U.S.-India Business Council into the U.S. Chamber.</p><p>  Before j
 oining the Chamber\, Choksy was a director at Chemonics International. The
 re\, he led teams developing economic ties between the United States and t
 he Middle East. He lived and worked in Jordan for seven years\, and also l
 ived and worked in India\, Sri Lanka\, and the U.K. Prior to that\, Choksy
  worked for the Parsons Corporation. He is a recognized thought leader on 
 the role of international business in the global economy.</p><p>  Choksy r
 eceived his M.B.A. in strategic management from the Wharton School of the 
 University of Pennsylvania and his B.A.\, magna cum laude\, from Hamilton 
 College. He lives with his wife and daughter in Chevy Chase\, Maryland.</p
 >
