DESCRIPTION:What does it actually take to build a career in intelligence o
 r the White House?  Join a small\, off-the-record dinner with Judd Devermo
 nt\, former National Intelligence Officer and senior National Security Cou
 ncil official who helped shape U.S. policy at the highest levels of govern
 ment.This is not a speech. It is a direct conversation.  He has worked ins
 ide the intelligence community\, coordinated across agencies\, and advised
  senior decision-makers on complex global crises. He has seen how power mo
 ves\, how policy is negotiated\, and how careers are built or stalled.We w
 ill talk about:      Breaking into intelligence and national security     
      Moving between government\, analysis\, and policy roles          Nego
 tiation and coalition building in real-world settings          The risks\,
  trade-offs\, and personal decisions behind public service          What e
 arly-career professionals get wrong      If you are serious about a future
  in intelligence\, national security\, or global affairs\, this is a rare 
 chance to ask unfiltered questions in a closed-door setting.Space is limit
 ed.
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20260312T205504Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING - Room Number: 1122
URL:https://events.iu.edu/hls/event/2213050-off-the-record-dinner-on-caree
 rs-in-intelligence-nat
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p data-start="518" data-end="598"><strong>Wh
 at does it actually take to build a career in intelligence or the White Ho
 use?</strong></p><p data-start="600" data-end="791">  Join a small\, off-t
 he-record dinner with Judd Devermont\, former National Intelligence Office
 r and senior National Security Council official who helped shape U.S. poli
 cy at the highest levels of government.</p><p data-start="793" data-end="8
 43"><strong>This is not a speech. It is a direct conversation.</strong></p
 ><p data-start="845" data-end="1076">  He has worked inside the intelligen
 ce community\, coordinated across agencies\, and advised senior decision-m
 akers on complex global crises. He has seen how power moves\, how policy i
 s negotiated\, and how careers are built or stalled.</p><p data-start="107
 8" data-end="1097"><strong>We will talk about:</strong></p><ul data-start=
 "1099" data-end="1389"><li data-start="1099" data-end="1151"><p data-start
 ="1101" data-end="1151">      Breaking into intelligence and national secu
 rity    </p></li><li data-start="1152" data-end="1209"><p data-start="1154
 " data-end="1209">      Moving between government\, analysis\, and policy 
 roles    </p></li><li data-start="1210" data-end="1271"><p data-start="121
 2" data-end="1271">      Negotiation and coalition building in real-world 
 settings    </p></li><li data-start="1272" data-end="1343"><p data-start="
 1274" data-end="1343">      The risks\, trade-offs\, and personal decision
 s behind public service    </p></li><li data-start="1344" data-end="1389">
 <p data-start="1346" data-end="1389">      What early-career professionals
  get wrong    </p></li></ul><p data-start="1391" data-end="1555">  If you 
 are serious about a future in intelligence\, national security\, or global
  affairs\, this is a rare chance to ask unfiltered questions in a closed-d
 oor setting.</p><p data-start="1557" data-end="1589" data-is-last-node="" 
 data-is-only-node=""><em>Space is limited.</em></p>
