BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Arts & Humanities Institute": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2143350-the-declaration-of-independence-the-motives-the", "IU Bloomington Libraries": "https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2143350-the-declaration-of-independence-the-motives-the"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Arts & Humanities Institute": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2143350-the-declaration-of-independence-the-motives-the"}
X-SOURCE-ID:iu_arts_humanities
X-SOURCE:IU Arts & Humanities Institute, IU Bloomington Libraries
SUMMARY:The Declaration of Independence: The Motives\, the Moment
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260613
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260614
DTSTAMP:20260109T131509Z
UID:20260421T040000Z-2165441@events.iu.edu__20260613
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,729,727/29248_America250-bloomington.rev.1765915309.png
CATEGORIES:Art and museum exhibitions
DESCRIPTION:The Declaration of Independence: The Motives\, The Momentexami
 nes a crucial time in American history through the collections of the Lill
 y Library. The first printing of the Declaration of Independence\, one of 
 only twenty-six copies known today\, is the centerpiece of the exhibition.
  Printed immediately for distribution\, it is the source of all revolution
 ary era reprintings. The famous hand-written version was an official forma
 lity created later\, signed in August\, and stored away.Books\, pamphlets\
 , newspapers\, prints\, and letters allow viewers to explore key questions
  about this moment in time. How did a people proud to be in the British em
 pire come to believe independence was possible or even desirable? What ide
 as and historical events did they draw on to justify resistance to Great B
 ritain? What kinds of communication networks would be crucial for circulat
 ing the Declaration and countless other revolutionary documents throughout
  the colonies?
GEO:39.167909;-86.518974
LAST-MODIFIED:20260113T170612Z
LOCATION:LILLY LIBRARY
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2143350-the-declaration-of-independe
 nce-the-motives-the
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><i><span data-contrast="auto">The Declarat
 ion of Independence: The Motives\, The Moment</span></i><span data-contras
 t="auto">examines a crucial time in American history through the collectio
 ns of the Lilly Library. The first printing of the Declaration of Independ
 ence\, one of only twenty-six copies known today\, is the centerpiece of t
 he exhibition. Printed immediately for distribution\, it is the source of 
 all revolutionary era reprintings. The famous hand-written version was an 
 official formality created later\, signed in August\, and stored away.</sp
 an><span data-ccp-props="{}"></span></p><p><span data-contrast="auto">Book
 s\, pamphlets\, newspapers\, prints\, and letters allow viewers to explore
  key questions about this moment in time. How did a people proud to be in 
 the British empire come to believe independence was possible or even desir
 able? What ideas and historical events did they draw on to justify resista
 nce to Great Britain? What kinds of communication networks would be crucia
 l for circulating the Declaration and countless other revolutionary docume
 nts throughout the colonies?</span><span data-ccp-props="{}"></span></p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ALL-DAY:1
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  <strong>Erika Dowell</strong><br />\n  Lil
 ly Library Executive Associate Director and Curator of Modern Manuscripts<
 br />\n  <a href="mailto:edowell@iu.edu">edowell@iu.edu<br />\n  <br /></a
 >\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2165441
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,729\,727/29248_America250-bloomington.rev.17
 65915309.png
X-LIVEWHALE-REPEATS-UNTIL:2026-07-24 04:00:00
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>\n  As part of the <a href="https://go.iu.edu/Ameri
 ca250" target="_blank" rel="noopener">America 250</a> initiative\, the Lil
 ly Library is proud to present an exhibition exploring the Declaration of 
 Independence.\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Arts & Humanities Institute": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2213132-declaration-of-independence-curator-talks"}
X-SOURCE-ID:iu_arts_humanities
X-SOURCE:IU Arts & Humanities Institute
SUMMARY:Declaration of Independence Curator Talks
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260613
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260614
DTSTAMP:20260413T103620Z
UID:20260613T040000Z-2266984@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;VALUE=DATE:20260613
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,288,288/30077_declaration_title_LW_graphic.rev.1771256965
 .png
CATEGORIES:Art and museum exhibitions,Lectures, talks, seminars, and confe
 rences
DESCRIPTION:The Declaration of Independence: The Motives\, The Moment is a
 n exhibition exploring the journey taken by the nation's founders. How did
  colonial leaders go from loyal subjects of the British empire to risking 
 everything by becoming revolutionaries? Take an insightful tour of the exh
 ibition from the curator\, with up close looks at Revolutionary War era co
 rrespondence\, books and manuscripts that influenced the revolutionaries\,
  and media that spread the word about the declaration.  Light refreshments
  will be offered. Come learn something new about the nation's founding and
  have a snack in Indiana University's rare book library!  Tours are offere
 d at noon\, 1 p.m.\, and 2 p.m. RSVP requested for planning of refreshment
 s and staffing.
GEO:39.167909;-86.518974
LAST-MODIFIED:20260413T143620Z
LOCATION:LILLY LIBRARY
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2213132-declaration-of-independence-
 curator-talks
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><em>The Declaration of Independence: The M
 otives\, The Moment</em> is an exhibition exploring the journey taken by t
 he nation's founders. How did colonial leaders go from loyal subjects of t
 he British empire to risking everything by becoming revolutionaries? Take 
 an insightful tour of the exhibition from the curator\, with up close look
 s at Revolutionary War era correspondence\, books and manuscripts that inf
 luenced the revolutionaries\, and media that spread the word about the dec
 laration.</p><p>  Light refreshments will be offered. Come learn something
  new about the nation's founding and have a snack in Indiana University's 
 rare book library!</p><p>  Tours are offered at noon\, 1 p.m.\, and 2 p.m.
  RSVP requested for planning of refreshments and staffing.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ALL-DAY:1
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  <strong>Lilly Library</strong><br />\n  <a
  href="mailto:liblilly@iu.edu">liblilly@iu.edu<br />\n  <br /></a>\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2266984
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,288\,288/30077_declaration_title_LW_graphic.
 rev.1771256965.png
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>\n  See the Lilly Library's rare original copy of t
 he Declaration of Independence and learn all about it from Erika Dowell\, 
 curator of the library's 2026 exhibition about the nation's founding docum
 ent.\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-TAGS:America 250
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Bloomington Libraries": "https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2236741-micro-mythologies-world-mythology-made-miniature"}
X-SOURCE-ID:iu_libraries
X-SOURCE:IU Bloomington Libraries
SUMMARY:Micro-mythologies: World Mythology Made Miniature
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260613
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260614
DTSTAMP:20260312T103247Z
UID:20260421T040000Z-2236741@events.iu.edu__20260613
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,1900,1900/30479_micromythologies_LW_avatar.rev.1773325700
 .png
CATEGORIES:Art and museum exhibitions
DESCRIPTION:"Myths are the world&#8217\;s dreams."- Joseph Campbell.To bel
 ieve is to be human.One of the greatest forces known to humankind\,belief 
 is a universal impetus\, one that has raised and toppled temples\, instiga
 ted and eased wars\, and inspired written works\, both monumental and mini
 ature. This exhibition showcases a selection of minute materials that pert
 ain to mythologies and belief systems from across the world. From ancient 
 Mesopotamian myth to modern Native American spirituality\, belief transcen
 ds time and history and intertwines human existence within our world.Micro
 -Mythologies: World Mythology Made Miniature is a miniature book exhibitio
 n which seeks to highlight some of our oldest and most powerful stories\, 
 as well as those who preserved them.Mythological influence has shaped the 
 world around us. Whether that be through architecture\, art\, or literatur
 e\, myths serve as a testament to human cultural memory and the preservati
 on of intangible tradition. Myths pull back the curtain of the past and re
 unite us with the beliefs and stories of our ancestors.Most of these mater
 ials originate from the collection of prolific miniature book collector\, 
 Ruth Elizabeth Adomeit (1910-1996). Notable book artists highlighted inMic
 ro-Mythologies include Maryline Poole Adams\, Carol Cunningham\, Barbara J
 . Raheb\, Dianne Weiss\, and Gabrielle Fox.Micro-Mythologies: World Mythol
 ogy Made Miniature is curated by Curatorial Assistant Jake Gentry\, with c
 ounsel from Sarah McElroy Mitchell\, Curator of Religious Collections. In 
 addition\, exhibition design was done by Jenny Mack and graphic design by 
 David Orr. This exhibition is on display in the Lilly Library Ball Room th
 rough December 2026.
GEO:39.167909;-86.518974
LAST-MODIFIED:20260312T143247Z
LOCATION:LILLY LIBRARY - Room Number: Ball Room
URL:https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2236741-micro-mythologies-world-
 mythology-made-miniature
